WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.040
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:10.440
Today we are going to see how Unreal&nbsp;
Engine 5.2, a popular game development&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:17.340
platform puts up a clinic on simulations.&nbsp;
Now, I am sure you are asking, okay Doctor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:24.480
but what kind of simulations? And the answer&nbsp;
is that here is every single thing that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:31.200
can possibly imagine is simulated. This is&nbsp;
going to be real good. In a previous episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:37.980
we saw how it can recreate real people&nbsp;
and put them into virtual environments.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:45.780
And today, first, we are going to have a look at&nbsp;
this…yes, it can simulate how all this beautiful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:52.800
foliage would look like in reality. These&nbsp;
are not perfect renderings, reality looks a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:59.940
little different, however, they are extremely&nbsp;
convincing, and oh my… they run in real time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:07.200
So, the apperance is being simulated here.&nbsp;
Then, since all this runs in real time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:13.800
we can grab a controller, jump into this&nbsp;
virtual electric vehicle, we can control it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:21.420
so the movement of the vehicle is simulated,&nbsp;
and, look! It also interacts with the environment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:29.340
beautifully. That is also simulated. So much so&nbsp;
that even the interaction of the suspension with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:34.800
the environment is computed as well. And&nbsp;
here comes the Two Minute Papers special,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:43.740
simulating interaction with fluids. Yummy, I&nbsp;
absolutely love this. And it even simulates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:55.080
how this interaction would sound in reality.&nbsp;
Even the compression and deformation of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.080 --> 00:02:02.580
tires is simulated. Something that was possible&nbsp;
through this earlier paper from NVIDIA. 2 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:10.500
ago this was a paper, and now it is right there in&nbsp;
a product that we can use right now. Incredible.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:18.780
And since this is a virtual world, the rules of&nbsp;
the real world don’t apply. Only our rules. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:25.620
let’s refinish this car with a beautiful&nbsp;
opal material. Oh my goodness. Look at those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:31.920
specular interactions, my eyes are popping&nbsp;
out of my head they look so good. This is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:39.240
type of material where multiple layers, and even&nbsp;
iridescence is simulated. Now add some dirt to it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:48.780
And, look. Tire deformation too. And all this&nbsp;
in real time? Pinch me. What a time to be alive!

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:56.460
And when we drive, even the hand movement on the&nbsp;
wheel is shown. Now, we keep driving a bit longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:03.720
and…something happened. Did you catch&nbsp;
it? Look again. Something big happened.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:10.380
Do you see it? Well, up until this point,&nbsp;
artists created this environment by hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:17.400
but from this point on, the entire environment&nbsp;
is generated procedurally, in other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:25.020
nearly completely automatically. Look.&nbsp;
We can add to it so easily. And if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:30.060
don’t like this new part here, just&nbsp;
grab it and move it. Like that. Wow.

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:36.360
Now, in games like Fortnite, they are using their&nbsp;
new rendering system, called Lumen. Which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:43.260
capable of computing global illumination in real&nbsp;
time. That is … I don’t even know what to say.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:50.280
Global illumination means an intricate simulation&nbsp;
of light where even indirect illumination can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:57.300
appear. This is the bleeding effect of light as it&nbsp;
bounces through multiple diffuse surfaces and is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:05.100
beautiful phenomenon in nature, but unfortunately,&nbsp;
less so in many virtual worlds, because it is so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:12.660
slow and expensive to compute. But here, whenever&nbsp;
environment changes, its effect is now computed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:19.440
properly. And not just the direct effect of the&nbsp;
light that you see here, but the indirect effect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:26.520
in other words, how it bounces around in&nbsp;
the scene. This also changes. Fantastic.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:31.320
And this means that whenever a wall&nbsp;
is destroyed, the lighting of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:38.280
whole scene changes. Once again, not just direct&nbsp;
illumination, but indirect illumination as well.

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:43.080
And I hear you asking, okay Doctor,&nbsp;
the geometry is quite simple here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:52.440
can it also shoulder more complex scenes? And the&nbsp;
answer is, look here. This is a light transport&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:59.820
researcher’s dream. Just look at that. This&nbsp;
used to take hours, if not days to compute,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:07.140
and now, once again, real time. I cannot&nbsp;
believe it. We now live in a time where we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:13.380
can create little virtual worlds where the light&nbsp;
of every single candle is computed convincingly.

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:19.680
I am so happy to see all of these amazing&nbsp;
research works coming together in one place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:27.840
and in real time. Two Minute Papers exists because&nbsp;
I want to draw your attention to human ingenuity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:35.160
I want to show you what can be done, and that&nbsp;
you can do it too. And my goodness, isn’t this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:42.720
an amazing celebration of human ingenuity. Bravo!&nbsp;
My apologies for not being able to report on this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:48.660
as quickly as the keynote came out, but there is&nbsp;
just so much happening in the world of research,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:54.600
there is so much I want to share with you,&nbsp;
and fortunately, progress is so quick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.600 --> 00:06:01.140
sometimes I don’t even know where to start.&nbsp;
We have a ton more amazing works coming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:06.960
so if you liked this one, consider subscribing&nbsp;
and hitting the bell icon to not miss them.

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:59.400
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

